{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Snowbank Zone: Nitrogen Fixers of a Thawing Yedoma Riverbank",
    "date": "2026-10-19",
    "lesson": "Along Siberia's Kolyma River, Pleistocene-age yedoma permafrost is collapsing into the water for the first time in ~30,000 years, releasing ancient organic carbon and locked nitrogen into a cold, dim river plume. Heterotrophic bacteria that specialize in protein-rich permafrost detritus bloom fast when thaw slumps slough sediment into the water, but the cold keeps everything sluggish: low Q10-scaled metabolism, slow swimming, and food that lingers because nothing degrades it quickly. The lesson is about a carbon bomb thawing in slow motion - abundant ancient substrate, but a community running near its thermal floor.",
    "citation": "Vonk et al. 2015, Biogeosciences; Mackelprang et al. 2011, Nature (permafrost metagenomics)",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "Kolyma River yedoma permafrost thaw slumps, NE Siberia, releasing Pleistocene organic matter into the river"
  },
